Blue-Green Deployment Architecture

Before looking at the architecture, remember one important rule:

In Blue-Green Deployment, both versions of the application are running at the same time, but users access only one version.

This is the biggest difference from a Rolling Update.

Complete Architecture
                    Internet / Users
                           │
                           ▼
                  Istio Ingress Gateway
                           │
                           ▼
                    VirtualService
                           │
                  Route to "blue" subset
                           │
                           ▼
                   DestinationRule
                  ┌─────────┴─────────┐
                  │                   │
             Blue Subset         Green Subset
           version=blue        version=green
                  │                   │
                  ▼                   ▼
         frontend-blue Pods    frontend-green Pods
                  │                   │
                  └─────────┬─────────┘
                            │
                            ▼
                  frontend Service (ClusterIP)
Let's Understand Every Component
1. User

The user opens your website.

For example:

http://myapp.com

The user has no idea whether Blue or Green is serving the request.

2. Istio Gateway

The request first reaches the Istio Ingress Gateway.

Think of it as the main entrance to your Kubernetes cluster.

Internet

↓

Istio Gateway

The Gateway's job is simple:

Accept external traffic
Forward it to the VirtualService

It does not decide which version gets traffic.

3. VirtualService

This is the brain of Blue-Green Deployment.

It contains the routing rules.

Initially it says:

100%

↓

Blue

So every request goes to Blue.

Tomorrow, when we switch versions, we won't touch the Deployments.

We'll only change this rule to:

100%

↓

Green

That single change redirects all user traffic.

4. DestinationRule

Now VirtualService says:

Send traffic to Blue.

But Kubernetes doesn't know what "Blue" means.

DestinationRule defines the subsets.

Example:

subsets:
- name: blue
  labels:
    version: blue

- name: green
  labels:
    version: green

This tells Istio:

Blue subset

=

Pods with

version=blue

and

Green subset

=

Pods with

version=green
5. Blue Deployment

Example:

frontend-blue

Pods:

frontend-blue-abc123

version=blue

This version is currently serving users.

6. Green Deployment

Example:

frontend-green

Pods:

frontend-green-def456

version=green

These Pods are already running and healthy.

But no production traffic reaches them yet.

Think of Green as being on standby.

7. Kubernetes Service

Many people get confused here.

There is only one Service.

frontend

Its selector is:

selector:
  app: frontend

Notice something important.

It does not include:

version: blue

or

version: green

Why?

Because the Service should include both Blue and Green Pods.

Istio will decide which subset receives traffic.

If the Service selected only version=blue, then Green Pods would never be reachable.

Complete Request Flow

Let's follow a real request.

Step 1

User opens:

myapp.com

↓

Step 2

Gateway accepts the request.

User

↓

Gateway

↓

Step 3

Gateway sends the request to VirtualService.

Gateway

↓

VirtualService

↓

Step 4

VirtualService checks its routing rule.

Destination:

Blue

↓

Step 5

DestinationRule translates:

Blue

=

version=blue

↓

Step 6

Istio selects only Pods having

version=blue

↓

Step 7

Blue Pod processes the request.

↓

Step 8

Response returns to the user.

Blue Pod

↓

Gateway

↓

User

Everything happens in milliseconds.

Internal Working

Now let's see what happens when you deploy a new version.

Phase 1 — Current Production

Blue is live.

Blue Pods

Running

Healthy

Receiving 100% traffic

Green does not exist yet (or is from an older release).

Phase 2 — Deploy New Version

Create Green.

frontend-green

Now Kubernetes starts:

Scheduling

↓

Pull Image

↓

Create Container

↓

Start Container

↓

Readiness Probe

↓

Healthy

Notice something important:

Users are still using Blue.

No one is affected.

Phase 3 — Verify Green

Before switching traffic, you can verify Green by checking:

Pods are Running
Readiness Probe passes
Logs look normal
Metrics are healthy
Internal test requests succeed

Because Green is already running, you can test it without impacting users.

Phase 4 — Switch Traffic

This is the key moment.

You do not:

Restart Pods
Delete Blue
Scale Blue down
Create a new ReplicaSet

Instead, you change one routing rule:

Before

route:
- destination:
    subset: blue

After

route:
- destination:
    subset: green

As soon as Istio receives the updated VirtualService, it pushes the new routing configuration to the Envoy proxies.

The next incoming request is sent to Green.

This is why the switch is effectively instantaneous.

Phase 5 — Rollback (if needed)

Suppose users report a bug immediately after the switch.

Do you need to redeploy?

No.

Do you need to recreate Pods?

No.

You simply update the VirtualService back to:

subset: blue

Traffic immediately returns to Blue because those Pods never stopped running.

Why Blue-Green Rollback Is So Fast

The old environment is still alive.

Blue

✓ Running

✓ Healthy

✓ Ready

Green is also running.

Only the traffic path changes.

That's why rollback often takes seconds, not minutes.

